    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d928">

                <pb facs="1049" n="945" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg945"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">823.00 Revolutions/326: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Burdett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lima</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-07-09T14:00:00-05:00">July 9, 1932—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>99. Trujillo bombed from air yesterday and prominent citizens telegraphed
                    Government July 9, 1 p.m. offering surrender of city to avoid further bombing.
                    Troops after capturing Salaverry are advancing on rebels from three sides and
                    revolt should be promptly subdued if troops continue loyal.</p>
                <p>The Embassy is attempting to communicate with Whyte<anchor n="2" xml:id="d928-f1"
                    /> concerning safety of Americans. Nothing to indicate they have been harmed.
                    Panagra aviator reports that nothing abnormal in appearance at Cartavio from the
                    air. Press despatches from Lima are being strictly censored.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Burdett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d928-f1" n="2" xml:id="d928-f1-content">
                <p> Neil Whyte, Consular Agent at Salaverry.</p>
